The failure was a UB overflow at TILE=2048: my "peak-live-tiles" model was wrong. The compiler statically allocates a UB slot for **every distinct SSA tile** (×unroll), not just the simultaneously-live set. The measured 353024 B (f16) / 328448 B (f32) at TILE=2048 is the ground truth I must scale from.

Key recalibration using the actual measurement (UB scales linearly with TILE, f16 is the binding case at 353024 B for T=2048):
- T=2048 → 353024 B (overflow, observed)
- T=1408 → ~242 KB (≈11 KB margin, ~4.6% — too thin)
- **T=1280 → ~220 KB (≈33 KB margin, 13% — solid)**
- T=1152 → ~199 KB (55 KB margin, 12.5% widening)

So TILE=1280 is the safe widening: a real ~25% wider tile vs 1024, with a measurement-grounded 33 KB margin. The `asc2.where`/compare destinations at T=1280 are 5120 B (5120 % 256 == 0), so the known destination-overrun defect is clear.

Correctness of the factored form is **provable, not just likely**: it computes exactly the same per-element values as the original (same `w=exp(-|x|)`, same per-branch num/den, same `y=x*tanh_sp`) — it only changes *when* the branch blend happens (blend num & den separately, then divide once, vs blend two quotients). For the selected branch the operands to `/` are identical, so the result is bit-identical to the proven 100% original. `asc2.full` is a listed standard op producing an exact 1.0 tile.

I'll keep the exact factored kernel (for which I have the UB measurement) and set `_TILE = 1280`.
